      "worked": "Since $\\sqrt{x^2+1}>0$, $y$ and $x-1$ must have the same sign, including zero.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y\\sqrt{x^2+1}&=x-1\\\\y^2(x^2+1)&=(x-1)^2\\\\(y^2-1)x^2+2x+(y^2-1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $y^2\\ne1$, this quadratic must have a real solution for $x$, so $\\Delta\\ge0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=4-4(y^2-1)^2\\\\\\Delta&=4y^2(2-y^2)\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis gives the candidate values $-\\sqrt2\\le y\\le\\sqrt2$. Squaring may have introduced extra values.\n\nWhen $y=\\pm1$, the rearranged equation gives $2x=0$, so $x=0$. The original equation then gives $y=-1$. Thus $y=-1$ is possible and $y=1$ is rejected.\n\nFor $x\\le1$, $y\\le0$. For $x>1$, $(x-1)^2<x^2+1$, so $0<y<1$. Hence the original function is always less than $1$.\n\nAt $x=-1$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt2}\\\\y&=-\\sqrt2.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe function is continuous for all real $x$ and approaches $1$ as $x$ increases without bound. Therefore every value between $-\\sqrt2$ and $1$ occurs, with $-\\sqrt2$ included and $1$ excluded.\n\nThe range is $\\boxed{[-\\sqrt2,1)}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
